      "model": "claude-fable-5-1",
      "question": "I've heard the ratio method can give odd answers when gas levels are low, so I want a second opinion on Transformer 1 at site MAIN. Using the average hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene over the available window, run the IEC 60599 ratio classification. Separately, work out the Duval Triangle 1 percentages from methane, ethylene and acetylene, and name the zone they fall in (PD, D1, D2, DT, T1, T2 or T3). Tell me whether the two methods agree. If they disagree, tell me which one you trust more and why, looking at whether the gases are high enough for ratios to mean anything. Then name the single most likely failure mode from our known transformer list. Use averages, not single samples. If the combined methane, ethylene and acetylene is under 10 ppm, say that the Duval result isn't meaningful and rely on the ratio result alone. If methane or ethylene has no data at all, say that neither method can be applied reliably and give a key-gas view from the largest gas instead.",
      "answer": "Nothing further is needed \u2014 all five gas averages are available (5 samples each, 2024\u201101\u201115 \u2192 2024\u201101\u201119), so I computed both classifications directly from the averages.\n\n**Average gases (ppm):** H\u2082 2,869 \u00b7 CH\u2084 7,331 \u00b7 C\u2082H\u2086 5,457 \u00b7 C\u2082H\u2084 16,627 \u00b7 C\u2082H\u2082 7\n\n**1. IEC 60599 ratio method**\n- C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 = 7 / 16,627 = **0.0004** \u2192 code 0 (<0.1)\n- CH\u2084/H\u2082 = 7,331 / 2,869 = **2.56** \u2192 code 2 (>1)\n- C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 = 16,627 / 5,457 = **3.05** \u2192 1\u20134 band\n- Classification: **T2 \u2013 thermal fault, 300\u2013700 \u00b0C** (no arcing signature). Note the 3.05 ratio is close to the T3 threshold (>4), so this is a borderline T2/T3 call.\n\n**2. Duval Triangle 1** (CH\u2084 + C\u2082H\u2084 + C\u2082H\u2082 = 23,965 ppm \u2014 well above 10 ppm, so the result is meaningful)\n- %CH\u2084 = **30.6 %**\n- %C\u2082H\u2084 = **69.4 %**\n- %C\u2082H\u2082 = **0.03 %**\n- Zone: **T3 \u2013 thermal fault > 700 \u00b0C** (C\u2082H\u2084 > 50 %, C\u2082H\u2082 < 15 %).\n\n**3. Agreement?** Partial. Both methods agree on a purely thermal fault with essentially no discharge/arcing, but they disagree on severity: IEC says T2, Duval says T3.\n\n**Which I trust more:** Duval T3. Your concern about low gas levels doesn't apply here \u2014 the gases are extremely high (ethylene ~16,600 ppm vs. typical IEC 90th\u2011percentile limits of a few hundred ppm; hydrogen and methane are also far above alarm levels), so the ratios are statistically meaningful. The disagreement comes from the IEC C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 ratio sitting near a hard threshold, whereas Duval reads the dominant ethylene fraction (69 %) directly, which is the classic signature of a >700 \u00b0C hot spot. Duval also doesn't suffer from \"no diagnosis\"/boundary artefacts. So: high\u2011temperature thermal fault, likely >700 \u00b0C, with no discharge activity.\n\n**Most likely failure mode (from the known transformer list):** **Thermal Fault \u2014 Core/Winding** \u2014 a T3 hot spot >700 \u00b0C is characteristic of overheating metal (core circulating currents, bad joints/contacts, or winding hot spots) rather than oil-only overheating. Given the gas magnitudes, this warrants urgent investigation (trend/retest, consider outage and internal inspection).",
      "score": {
